; Two parameters of one function may not share a name.
;
; `pushScope` binds them in order, so a reference resolves to the LAST
; and the earlier one is unreachable - its argument is evaluated and
; discarded, which is the same defect AX3020 refuses for a macro's
; parameters. The louder half is codegen: it names an LLVM register
; after each parameter, so two of a name emit `%x` twice. That is a
; well-typed, `check`-clean program no LLVM tool will accept, and the
; driver reports it as `AX4003 opt failed` against `<toolchain>` with
; no span into the user's file - the compiler blaming the toolchain for
; its own output.
;
; `_` is NOT exempt, and that is a real difference from AX3020's rule.
; A macro pattern's `_` binds nothing, so several are free; a function
; parameter always binds a register whatever it is called, so a second
; `_` collides exactly as a second `x` does. The idiomatic ML spelling
; `(fn (f _ _) 0)` has to be written `(fn (f _a _b) 0)` here.
;
; Making `_` a fresh anonymous binder instead is the better end state
; and costs a codegen change - each occurrence would need its own
; register name - which is worth doing when something in the tree wants
; it. The corpus population of `_` as a function parameter is 0, so
; nothing does today.
;
; It reuses AX3006 rather than taking a number: "this name is bound
; twice here" is the claim that code already makes, and a parameter is
; a binding.

; Refused: `x` twice.
(:: dup (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (dup x x) x)

; Refused: `_` twice, for the same reason.
(:: ignoreBoth (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (ignoreBoth _ _) 0)

; Accepted, and unchanged: one `_` binds one register.
(:: ignoreOne (-> Int Int))
(fn (ignoreOne _) 7)

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (ignoreOne 1))
